---
onetaskgraph_template: 1
description: >-
  The design document a plan is read and approved as. The variables below are its data; how
  to write each answer is stated in this template's guidance comments, which no rendering
  carries.
variables:
  what:
    description: >-
      The What section's prose.
    type: text
    required: true
  why:
    description: >-
      The Why section's prose.
    type: text
    required: true
  architecture:
    description: >-
      The Architecture section's overview only. Each unit's subsection is rendered from
      `units`.
    type: text
    required: true
  units:
    description: >-
      One object per unit that changes, in reading order, with these keys: `name` (string,
      the unit's label, which a planned task's `unit` names); `repository` (string, the
      repository's short name, such as `onepipeline`); `part` (string, the package or path
      within that repository, or `""` when the unit is the whole repository); `summary`
      (text, the unit's one paragraph); `reversible` (a list of objects, the unit's trivial and
      low-cost decisions in reading order, or `[]` when it has none), each with the string
      keys `title` (its short bold title) and `text` (its one sentence); and `decisions` (a
      list of objects, the unit's high-cost decisions, or `[]` when it has none). Each decision has the keys `name`
      (string, its heading), `justification` (string, naming who would have to act to undo
      it), `summary` (text, its plain meaning) and `artifact` (text, one or more fenced
      blocks, or `""` for a behavioural decision). The template renders each decision's
      reversibility line from its `justification`; the level is not an answer.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  acceptance_criteria:
    description: >-
      One criterion per entry, each a string.
    type: list
    items: string
    required: true
  planned_tasks:
    description: >-
      One object per task of the plan, in the order the table lists them, with five string
      keys: `task` (its title), `unit` (the `name` of the unit it changes), `delivers` (what
      it delivers), `depends_on` (the titles it depends on, or `none`) and `location` (where
      the task lives). Each value is rendered as one table cell on one line.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  workload:
    description: >-
      The realistic usage the plan's budgets hold at, with numbers: data volume and shape,
      concurrency, items per run.
    type: text
    required: false
    default: ""
  checklist:
    description: >-
      One object per concern the plan was checked against, with three string keys:
      `concern` (the concern, such as latency or gate time), `budget` (the `id` of the entry
      of `budgets` that covers it) and `not_applicable` (a one-line "n/a because ..."). Exactly
      one of `budget` and `not_applicable` is non-empty; the other is `""`.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
  ten_x:
    description: >-
      The answer to "at 10x realistic usage, what does the product owner notice getting worse
      first?", naming the `id` of the budget that covers it or saying why none does.
    type: text
    required: false
    default: ""
  # llmlint: ignore-block[contracts_have_one_source_or_a_drift_gate] The `budgets` description: this restates the onebudgetspec keys a plan's budgets document names, which the task that added it requires; their reconciliation with the installed library is the `aio-budgets` node's, which adopts that library here, and the vocabularies in it are held to `orchestrator/plan_budgets.py` by `tests/test_plan_budgets_template.py` and `tests/test_plan_budgets.py`.
  budgets:
    description: >-
      One object per budget the plan proposes, or `[]` when it proposes none, with these
      keys. `id` (string, the budget's id in its budgets file). `repository` (string, the
      normalized origin `host/owner/name` of the repository the budget goes in). `file`
      (string, the repository-relative path of the `budgets.yaml` it goes in: `budgets.yaml`
      for a repo-wide budget, or a project's own file for a scoped one). `measure` (string,
      what is measured, in words, as near as possible to where the product owner feels it).
      `inner_measure_reason` (string, why the measure is taken further inward than that, or
      `""` when it is not). `unit` (string), `direction` (string, `max` or `min`) and
      `threshold` (a number), each meaning what it means in onebudgetspec. `workload`
      (string, the realistic workload the threshold holds at, which is never written into
      the budgets file). `evidence` (string, where the number came from: a spike report's id
      and figures, or the measurement it rests on). `command` (string, the command that
      performs the measurement, as it will be registered). `node` (string, the id of the plan
      node that owns the budget's command). `file_change` (string, `add`, `change` or
      `none`).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
  # llmlint: ignore-end[contracts_have_one_source_or_a_drift_gate]
  repo_wide_effects:
    description: >-
      At least one object per repository the plan's tasks change, with three string keys:
      `repository` (its normalized origin), `budget` (the `id` of a budget in that
      repository's root `budgets.yaml`, or `""` when it has none) and `effect` (the expected
      effect of the plan on it, never empty, and `none` when there is none).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
  realistic_data:
    description: >-
      One object per kind of data the plan makes realistic, or `[]`, with four string keys:
      `data` (what the data is), `choice` (`generator`, `fixture`, `hybrid` or
      `real-sample`), `reason` (why, weighed on realism, generation cost, fixture cost and
      upkeep) and `artifact` (the repository artifact spikes, checks and later plans share).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
  spike_findings:
    description: >-
      One object per spike finding that changed the plan, or `[]`, with three string keys:
      `spike` (the spike's id), `finding` (what it found) and `changed` (what in the plan it
      changed).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
  predates_budgets:
    description: >-
      The reason `config/budgets-migration.yaml` gives for this plan when it names the plan,
      which then carries no budgets document; `""` for every other plan.
    type: text
    required: false
    default: ""
---
{#- A table cell is one line holding no bare column delimiter, whatever an answer says. -#}
{% macro cell(value) %}{{ value | replace("|", "\\|") | replace("\r\n", " ") | replace("\n", " ") | replace("\r", " ") }}{% endmacro %}
{#- A budget's target in words: the direction, the threshold and the unit. -#}
{% macro target(budget) %}{% if budget.direction == "max" %}at most{% elif budget.direction == "min" %}at least{% else %}{{ budget.direction }}{% endif %} {{ budget.threshold }} {{ budget.unit }}{% endmacro %}
{% block document_guidance %}
{#
  THE READER. A technical product manager with no depth in this domain. They read this
  document instead of the plan, and must be able to accept a hard-to-reverse decision, argue
  with the architecture, or say the acceptance criteria do not describe what was asked.

  PROSE. Plain, simple, terse and precise, and no more technical than the plan's own goal
  statement. A term that cannot be avoided is defined once, in passing.

  LENGTH. A document is about as long as the design document this template replaces would
  be for the same plan. Exactness is spent on what is hard to undo, and everything else is
  said in a sentence.

  SECTIONS. In this order: What, Why, Architecture, then the four budget sections —
  Budgets, Budgets-file changes, Realistic data, Spike findings — then Acceptance criteria
  and Planned tasks. A plan the migration list names renders one line in place of the four
  budget sections. There is no separate Contracts section, and no answer adds a heading of
  its own.

  HIGHLIGHTING. No GitHub alert blocks (`> [!CAUTION]` and the like) anywhere. The bold
  reversibility line is the whole of the highlighting.

  THIS TEMPLATE. Each section renders inside its own block: `what`, `why`, `architecture`,
  `budgets`, `budget_files`, `realistic_data`, `spike_findings`, `acceptance_criteria` and
  `planned_tasks`. Each section's guidance is a Jinja comment in a
  separate block, `<name>_guidance`, and this block, `document_guidance`, holds what applies
  to the whole document. Guidance is always a Jinja comment, never an HTML comment, so a
  rendered document carries none of it. The front matter states the data shape, what each
  answer and key holds; the judgment lives here. A repository overrides any one block, a
  section's guidance without its rendering or the other way round, through the engine's
  repository layer: `.onepipeline/templates/design-doc.md.j2` in its checkout, a file
  beginning `{% extends "onepipeline/host/design-doc.md.j2" %}` that redefines only the
  blocks it names.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block what_guidance %}
{#
  WHAT. What is being built. What and Why are each a few plain sentences in the user's
  terms, and carry no interface detail and no implementation detail.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block what %}
## What

{{ what }}

{% endblock %}
{% block why_guidance %}
{#
  WHY. Why the user wants it, in their own words where the plan records them.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block why %}
## Why

{{ why }}

{% endblock %}
{% block architecture_guidance %}
{#
  ARCHITECTURE. The `architecture` answer is a short overview, two to four sentences saying
  how the units fit. A unit is a repository, or a part of a project. Every unit that changes
  gets one subsection, headed `### <unit> — `<repository>` (`<package or path>`)`; the
  parenthesis appears only when the unit is part of a repository.

  Under each unit heading:
    - One paragraph. It says in plain words what the unit is and what changes in it. It
      does not list the unit's individual decisions; those follow.
    - One `#### <decision>` per high-cost decision the unit owns, and only for those, so a
      unit with none has no `####`. Each opens with `**Reversibility: high cost** —` and a
      justification naming who would have to act to undo it. Then one or two plain
      sentences say what the decision means. Then the literal artifact: a `diff` block for
      a changed shape, the full shape for a new one, a `--help` diff for a CLI, a text
      sketch for a UI. A behavioural decision with no artifact says so in one sentence.
    - Then, after the high-cost subsections, a `**Reversible:**` line followed by one
      bullet per trivial or low-cost decision the unit makes: an interface, a stored shape,
      a flag, a behaviour. Each bullet is `- **<short title>.** <one sentence>`, and the
      sentence names the thing exactly (the flag, key, field, route or command). A bullet
      carries no level label, no artifact and no justification, because trivial and low
      cost read the same here. A unit with no such decision has no `**Reversible:**` line.
    - Nothing else: no Kind, Owner or Status line, no Owes or May-assume bullets, and no
      links between sections.

  REVERSIBILITY. Three levels, defined by who would have to act to undo the decision:
    - trivial: only the unit that owns it, in one ordinary change.
    - low cost: parties we control, changing together. For example, several of our
      repositories or tools updated in step, a deprecation window, or a migration of data
      we own.
    - high cost: parties we don't control or can't list, or data we can't rewrite. For
      example, a public flag or field other people script against, a released artifact, or
      stored data that older installed versions or outside readers keep reading.
  When in doubt, the stricter level. These definitions are guidance a repository may
  override by replacing this block.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block architecture %}
## Architecture

{{ architecture }}

{% for unit in units %}
### {{ unit.name }} — `{{ unit.repository }}`{% if unit.part %} (`{{ unit.part }}`){% endif %}


{{ unit.summary }}

{% for decision in unit.decisions %}
#### {{ decision.name }}

**Reversibility: high cost** — {{ decision.justification }}

{{ decision.summary }}

{% if decision.artifact %}
{{ decision.artifact }}

{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if unit.reversible %}
**Reversible:**

{% for item in unit.reversible %}
- **{{ item.title }}.** {{ item.text | replace("\n", "\n  ") }}
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endblock %}
{% block budgets_guidance %}
{#
  BUDGETS. The budget answers — `workload`, `checklist`, `ten_x`, `budgets`,
  `repo_wide_effects`, `realistic_data` and `spike_findings` — restate the plan's budgets
  document, the project document `<project>-budgets`, answer for answer: copy each from the
  answers that document was rendered from, and invent none. A plan the migration list
  `config/budgets-migration.yaml` names carries no budgets document; for it, answer
  `predates_budgets` with the reason the list gives, word for word, and leave every budget
  answer empty, and the section reads "This plan predates budgets: <reason>" in place of
  all four budget sections.

  The section opens with the realistic workload in the plan's own numbers, then a table with
  one row per budget: the measure, the realistic workload it holds at, the target, the spike
  evidence the number rests on, and the check — the command that performs the measurement
  and the budgets file it is registered in. Then the answer to what the product owner
  notices getting worse first at 10x realistic usage, and the concerns the plan found need
  no budget, each with its reason. A reader approving this approves these targets.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block budgets %}
{% if predates_budgets %}
## Budgets

This plan predates budgets: {{ predates_budgets }}

{% elif workload or checklist or ten_x or budgets or repo_wide_effects or realistic_data or spike_findings %}
## Budgets

{{ workload }}

| Measure | Realistic workload | Target | Spike evidence | Check |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
{% for budget in budgets %}
| {{ cell(budget.measure) }} | {{ cell(budget.workload) }} | {{ cell(target(budget)) }} | {{ cell(budget.evidence) }} | `{{ cell(budget.command) }}` in `{{ cell(budget.file) }}` |
{% endfor %}

**At 10x realistic usage:** {{ ten_x }}

{% for entry in checklist if entry.not_applicable %}
{% if loop.first %}
**Not budgeted:**

{% endif %}
- **{{ entry.concern }}.** {{ entry.not_applicable }}
{% if loop.last %}

{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
{% block budget_files_guidance %}
{#
  BUDGETS-FILE CHANGES. One subsection per repository the plan changes, from
  `repo_wide_effects`: the budgets the plan adds to or changes in that repository's budgets
  files, each with the file it goes in, and then the expected effect on each of its
  repo-wide budgets. The effect is stated even when it is "none", because approving this
  document is what approves a change to a budgets file, and nothing else changes one.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block budget_files %}
{% if repo_wide_effects and not predates_budgets %}
## Budgets-file changes

{% for repository in repo_wide_effects | map(attribute="repository") | unique %}
### `{{ repository }}`

{% for budget in budgets | selectattr("repository", "equalto", repository) %}
- **`{{ budget.id }}`** — {{ budget.file_change }} in `{{ budget.file }}`.
{% else %}
- No budget of this plan goes in its budgets files.
{% endfor %}
{% for entry in repo_wide_effects | selectattr("repository", "equalto", repository) %}
- **Repo-wide{% if entry.budget %} `{{ entry.budget }}`{% endif %}:** {{ entry.effect }}
{% endfor %}

{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
{% block realistic_data_guidance %}
{#
  REALISTIC DATA. One bullet per kind of data the plan makes realistic, from
  `realistic_data`: the choice — a generator, a fixture, a hybrid or a real sample — and the
  reason, weighed on realism, generation cost, fixture cost and upkeep, naming the artifact
  that holds it.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block realistic_data %}
{% if realistic_data and not predates_budgets %}
## Realistic data

{% for entry in realistic_data %}
- **{{ entry.data }}:** {{ entry.choice }}. {{ entry.reason }} (`{{ entry.artifact }}`)
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
{% block spike_findings_guidance %}
{#
  SPIKE FINDINGS. One bullet per spike finding that changed the plan, from
  `spike_findings`, stated plainly: what the spike found, and what in the plan it changed.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block spike_findings %}
{% if spike_findings and not predates_budgets %}
## Spike findings

{% for entry in spike_findings %}
- **{{ entry.spike }}:** {{ entry.finding }} So {{ entry.changed }}
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
{% block acceptance_criteria_guidance %}
{#
  ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA. A plain list of what must be observably true when the plan is done,
  no longer than the plan warrants.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block acceptance_criteria %}
## Acceptance criteria

{% for criterion in acceptance_criteria %}
- {{ criterion | replace("\n", "\n  ") }}
{% endfor %}

{% endblock %}
{% block planned_tasks_guidance %}
{#
  PLANNED TASKS. A table with the columns Task, Unit, What it delivers, Depends on, Where it
  lives: one row per task, every cell one line, and "What it delivers" a short phrase. The
  Unit column names the unit the task changes. Where it lives is the location the plan store
  reports for the task, never one composed by hand.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block planned_tasks %}
## Planned tasks

| Task | Unit | What it delivers | Depends on | Where it lives |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
{% for row in planned_tasks %}
| {{ cell(row.task) }} | {{ cell(row.unit) }} | {{ cell(row.delivers) }} | {{ cell(row.depends_on or "none") }} | {{ cell(row.location) }} |
{% endfor %}
{% endblock %}
